        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 736,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 818,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "mgl_color sets the colour of everything drawn after it.\n\nIT IS PUT BACK, LIKE A TRANSFORM:\nA colour applies from where it sits onward and is restored at the end of its\nbranch. Before any mgl_color, the colour is white.\n\nCOLOUR TINTS, LIGHT SHADES:\nThe colour multiplies the lit surface, so mgl_color sets the hue and the\nshading stays. The highlight is not tinted - a red sphere still has a white\nglint. mgl_line_array and mgl_text are not lit at all, and simply draw in\nthe current colour.\n\nThe alpha is the shape's transparency.\n\nTHE NUMBERS:\nRed green blue alpha, either from 0 to 1 or from 0 to 255 - a colour with\nany value above 1 is read as 0 to 255. Three numbers take alpha as full.\n\nOne trap: the colour picker keeps its alpha at 255 when it is handed three\nnumbers, so a 0 to 1 colour sent as three numbers is saved with an alpha of\n255, and comes back nearly black when the patch is opened again. Send four\nnumbers, or 0 to 255.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_color\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_color\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. This is what triggers the node.\n\ncolor:\nThe colour, as a list of three or four numbers or from the picker.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, with this colour in force downstream.\n\nRELATED:\nmgl_material for how surfaces answer light. gl_color is the gl equivalent.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "mgl_color sets the colour of everything drawn after it.\n\nIT IS PUT BACK, LIKE A TRANSFORM:\nA colour applies from where it sits onward and is restored at the end of its\nbranch. Before any mgl_color, the colour is white.\n\nCOLOUR TINTS, LIGHT SHADES:\nThe colour multiplies the lit surface, so mgl_color sets the hue and the\nshading stays. The highlight is not tinted - a red sphere still has a white\nglint. mgl_line_array and mgl_text are not lit at all, and simply draw in\nthe current colour.\n\nThe alpha is the shape's transparency.\n\nTHE NUMBERS:\nRed green blue alpha, either from 0 to 1 or from 0 to 255 - a colour with\nany value above 1 is read as 0 to 255. Three numbers take alpha as full.\n\nOne trap: the colour picker keeps its alpha at 255 when it is handed three\nnumbers, so a 0 to 1 colour sent as three numbers is saved with an alpha of\n255, and comes back nearly black when the patch is opened again. Send four\nnumbers, or 0 to 255.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_color\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_color\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. This is what triggers the node.\n\ncolor:\nThe colour, as a list of three or four numbers or from the picker.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, with this colour in force downstream.\n\nRELATED:\nmgl_material for how surfaces answer light. gl_color is the gl equivalent."
        },
